105
00:07:36,740 --> 00:07:43,660
 So basically, the gist of this is that the Git, when it's parsing through like a Git config file,

106
00:07:43,660 --> 00:07:48,700
 it will parse the different sections according to, you know, different characters.

107
00:07:48,700 --> 00:07:50,940
 So it'll check, does it start with like an open bracket?

108
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:53,680
 That would signify that it's a section, that type of stuff.

109
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:58,940
 But what Andre realized is that as it's reading through this, it reads 1024 bytes at a time.

110
00:07:58,940 --> 00:08:04,740
 And basically, like, there's not a buffer overflow there because it's like checking how much it's reading.

111
00:08:04,740 --> 00:08:08,300
 However, the way that it's parsing it leads to other vulnerabilities.

112
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:13,380
 So essentially, you can create a line that's over 1024 characters.

113
00:08:13,380 --> 00:08:18,700
 When it reads the 1024, it gets halfway through that line, very similar to the bug that we exploded.

114
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:22,320
 Dang, this is very similar to the bug that we just exploded.

115
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:28,540
 And then it starts again, like halfway through that line after 1024 characters and reads the rest of it,

116
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:34,400
 which then lets you basically create like a bogus section within there that adds it in.

117
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:39,720
 And so through sections, you can have it run different commands and all that kind of stuff.

118
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:45,860
 So you basically overwrite like the core section and have it run a command when you do like a Git submodule update or something.

119
00:08:46,100 --> 00:08:48,560
 So it's a really, really cool bug.

